258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (1. Strategic Visibility within Visitor Movement)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [240]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (Information should be positioned within the natural line of sight and movement flow of visitors, particularly at pause points and transition zones. Rather than being placed in peripheral or passive locations, information must be intentionally integrated into navigation paths so that it becomes difficult to overlook. Strategic placement ensures that visitors encounter information naturally as part of their spatial journey.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [241]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (2. Glance-Friendly and Layered Information Design)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [242]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (Visitors in public spaces rarely engage in prolonged reading. Therefore, information should be visually striking yet cognitively light, designed for quick scanning rather than deep reading. Content should be structured into layered, bite-sized modules, allowing visitors to move from headline insights to deeper information progressively, supporting natural skim-to-engage behaviour.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [243]
>>
endobj
262 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (3. Multi-Sensory Information Systems \(Audio + Visual\))
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [244]
>>
endobj
263 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (In open and high-distraction environments, static signage alone often fails to capture attention. Audio storytelling can act as a primary attention trigger, while visual information reinforces comprehension. Synchronising audio cues with nearby visual elements creates multi-sensory redundancy, improving engagement, recall, and comprehension.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [245]
>>
endobj
264 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (4. Social and Curiosity-Driven Engagement)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [246]
>>
endobj
265 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (Visitor behaviour is strongly influenced by curiosity and social cues. Designing exhibits that create moments of intrigue, discovery, or spectacle encourages visitors to pause and explore. When engagement becomes visible and repeatable within the space, it triggers herd behaviour where others follow the same interaction patterns, increasing collective participation.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [247]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (5. Spatial Storytelling and Immersive Information Environments)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [248]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17 0 R
/E (Information should not exist as isolated boards but as part of a larger spatial narrative. Environmental cues such as landscape transitions, thematic zoning, markers, and contextual storytelling can transform the space into an immersive learning environment. In this approach, information becomes embedded within the experience of the space rather than functioning as separate instructional content.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [249]
>>
endobj
